OpenTSense (G94, 17 Nov 2026): HotSpot's SuperLU backward Euler, applied on top of hotspot-f18831e.patch in the
opentsense:g94b image only.
1. Reuse the LU factors of A = C/h + G between calls while G, C and h are unchanged (dgstrs), instead of a full
   dgssv factorisation every step; dgssv itself factorises and then solves with dgstrs, so each step's arithmetic is
   unchanged (equivalence against opentsense:g94a: gates/g94/dry/lu_check/compare.txt). On the 3d stack the unpatched
   build took about 90 s per 5 ms step at a 2.9 GB container cap (lu_check/hs1.sh); the G94 dry run's level 0 ran
   27+ min before it was stopped.
2. Free what each step allocated and never freed: the right-hand side's values and the two SuperMatrix structs in
   backward_euler, and the power vector in compute_temp_grid (two n-vectors per step, about 1 MB each on the 3d
   stack: about 20 GB at 10,000 steps).
3. Stop with an error when SuperLU reports one (the return code was ignored).
4. Free the cached factors before the steady-state solve that HotSpot always runs after a transient trace
   (steady_state_temp_grid), so two factorisations are never held at once: without this the 3d stack's run went over
   a 2.9 GB container cap that the unpatched build stays under.
The model is unchanged.
